 "unfinished": {
  "_note": "Is a question finished? (site 2.9, 17 November 2026. The senior author typed 'can i die from ' and was told 'We have no answer to this. Pharmakon answers questions about drugs': 'it should understand and correctly diagnose every instance and meaning of human language'.) By its words alone, the same on the page (insUnfinished) and in the build (Ranker.unfinished), with each drug's name read as 'it'. A question is NOT finished when: (ends) its last word is one a question cannot end on ('can i die from', 'weed and', 'what is the best way to'); (bare) it names a drug, or is asked on a drug's page, and holds nothing but words of 'aux' ('is cocaine', 'how does mdma'); (which) it names no drug, is not asked on a drug's page, and every word is in 'aux', 'func' or 'topic' ('is it addictive', 'can you overdose', 'how long does it last': what it is about has not been said), unless it has no word of 'gap' and holds a word of 'nouns', or begins with 'why' and holds a word of 'topic' ('how long does withdrawal last', 'why do people get addicted': general questions); (typing) its last word is not a word (not in the model's vocabulary, not in these lists, not a known word without its ending) and is the beginning of a drug's name ('can i die from coc') or, in a question that otherwise reads as one about drugs, of a word of these lists ('is weed lega'). 'strand': patterns of questions that may end on such a word ('what are pills cut with', 'what is a comedown like'); 'whole': patterns of questions that are finished all the same ('what is tolerance', 'who are you'). Two drugs' names joined by words of 'join' are a finished question ('weed and alcohol'); 'named': patterns that are finished when the 'it' is a drug's name ('what is ketamine', 'what is ketamine for'). A question that is not finished gets no verdict and no way to send it: the page asks which drug is meant, or to go on, and shows the nearest answers; scripts/auto/ask.py never lists one. Measured by scripts/insight/build_index.py --words (no model needed): how many real forum questions the rule wrongly calls unfinished, and how many questions cut off before the drug's name it catches. (site 2.9.2, 17 November 2026: the senior author typed 'do gay people' and was told 'This does not look like a question about drugs': 'we have to include our site responding perfectly to every circumstance this is what smartness is about'.) Two more ways a question is NOT finished. (open) it names no drug and holds nothing but words of 'aux' and words for people ('who': people, women, my mom; 'whoadj': gay, older, pregnant): a subject, and nothing said of it ('do gay people', 'why do women', 'how many people'); with a drug named, or on a drug's page, the same is 'bare'. A word of 'be' before a last word of 'whoadj' says something of them ('are people gay'): finished. (verb) a helper verb ('help': do, does, can, should, will) needs a main verb in its base form, and there is none after it ('does smoking weed', 'how long does the high', 'can dogs'). By grammar, not by topic: a word can be the main verb when it stands right after a pronoun or a drug's name, is a word of 'verbs' or of 'be', or is a word whose -ing or -ed form the model's word list knows; never right after a word of 'det' ('the high'), never a word of 'aux', 'who' or 'whoadj', never a drug word; a word the model does not know may be a verb unless it has an ending. Before a word of 'do' only words of 'open' may stand ('why do', 'how long does'): after a subject, 'do' can be the main verb ('people do drugs'). Read only in questions of six words or fewer. The everyday words for people stay in 'func' too ('do people die': which drug?); a question with one of the other words for people and no word of 'gap' is about people, and finished ('how do i get a girlfriend'). 'pp': after a form of 'be', one of these and a word for people say something ('is weed for kids'): finished. 'wants': the words of 'ends' that call for a thing ('can i die from'): the page then asks which drug is meant; else it says to go on. 'short': the short forms people type ('u', 'ppl', 'wat', 'gonna'), read as the words they stand for before the rule reads the question; 'abbr': short words that are whole words although the model's word list lacks them ('bac', 'uk'): never taken for the beginning of a word.",
